      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Since \\[10x+2 = 2(5x+1), 10x+6 = 2(5x+3),\\]\nthe expression can be written as \\[2(5x+1)(5x+3)(5x+5).\\]\nNow, \\[5x+1 = 5x+1, 5x+3 = 5(x+1)+2 = 5(x+1)+2 ,\\]\n\\[ \\text{and}, \\ 5x+5 = 5x+5 = 5(x+1),\\ so, \\]\n\\[ 2(5x+1)(5x+3)(5x+5) = 2(5x+1)(5x+2)(5x+5).\\]Let's call the expression \\[ E = 2(5x+1)(5x+2)(5x+5)\\[ Let's multiply $E$ by $5$:\n\\begin{align*}\nE \\cdot 5 &= 2(5x+1)(5x+2)(5x+5) \\cdot 5\\\\\n        &= 2(5(5x+1))(5(5x+2))(5(x+1))\\\\\n        &= 2*25^2 * (5x+1)(5x+2)(x+1)\\quad \\text{(since } 5^2 =25 )\n\\end{align*}We now have the product $2 \\cdot 25^2 (5x+1)(5x+2)(x+1)$.  Note that this expression will be divisible by $25$, since its product includes $25^2$. The largest integer dividing $E$ will therefore be its greatest common divisor with $25$.  \n\nNow, we need to check whether $E$ is divisible by $5$. Since $5x+1$, $5x+2$, and $5x+5$ are each at most $5$, then $E$ is at most $5^4$, which divides into $5$ evenly. Since $5$ divides into $5$ evenly, $5$ will be a divisor to our expression; we now know that the greatest common divisor with $25$ is $25$, since the GCD of any expression which has a divisor of $25$ and one which has a divisor of $5$ evenly is equal to whatever the larger of those divisors is. Since $25$ is roughly $4$ times smaller than $500$, we can thus say that our expression $E$ has the largest integer dividing it as $\\boxed{125}$.",
